  "assumptions": [
    "Illustration: a made-up two-setting loss bowl with simulated batch noise, not a real model trained on real data.",
    "Loss = (4x² + z²)/2, drawn at 0.25 × loss over x, z from -2 to 2. It is 4 times steeper in x, so steps overshoot in x first: above learning rate 0.5 the x swings grow; at exactly 0.5 they bounce back and forth.",
    "Step = −learning rate × (true slope + noise). Noise is random, centred on zero, with spread 3·√((64−b)/(63·b)) for batch b, so it is exactly zero at 64. A single step lowers the loss only on average.",
    "Both arrows start at the ball and are drawn at 2 × the step length. Orange: the actual mini-batch step. Grey: the step the full data would give. The step-length readout shows the orange step's true length.",
    "A run ends after 40 steps (finished) or when the loss goes past 8 (diverged). The trail and the ghost each hold up to 41 points: the start plus 40 steps.",
    "Noise values come from a fixed seed, so Reset replays the same noise. 'New run, keep last path' uses the next noise values, keeps the finished path as the ghost and starts stepping."
  ]
}